{"agent_id":"pp3g-7zS8-read-only-adversarial-review-r2-retry-kimi-k2-7-code","agent_seq":11,"timestamp":"2026-08-09T18:08:54.577701773Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"8bcd9b03-5c30-4db2-b7c8-95a842ec9a79","comment_uuid":"bb7b3ae7-108b-4f34-851c-5c8d0f522076","author":"pp3g-7zS8-read-only-adversarial-review-r2-retry-kimi-k2-7-code","content":"- Section 2.3 / 5.1: --allowedTools no-enforcement claim is overbroad. The document correctly notes the host wrapper drops --allowedTools for local tmux launches. But launch.rs lines 1023-1026 pass --allowedTools directly to the claude binary inside Docker/Podman containers without the host wrapper in the way. If the container image ships a real Claude Code binary or a wrapper that forwards it, the tool surface is enforced there. Fix: qualify the claim by launch path and container image behavior.\n- Section 3.2 vs 3.3: orchestrator-guard lacks the CROSSLINK_AGENT_TYPE env fallback that crosslink-guard uses. crosslink-guard resolves agent type via runtimeAgentBySession, then CROSSLINK_AGENT_TYPE env, then hook-config agent.type. orchestrator-guard only uses agentBySession and falls back to currentAgent, never the env. If a kickoff session's chat.params event is delayed or lost, a builder's write could be incorrectly blocked. Fix: align orchestrator-guard resolution with crosslink-guard or document the inconsistency.","created_at":"2026-08-09T18:08:54.568244610Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:rTYY4fhkDYtz6SJeo0n+sM+0Gvd3/0k1KjtJzKUGLS8","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg5xjAGNcgpxSjh5K/KArdIxgMLULCtBFGf6IsF2nPXyEAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAk6tH9skforr+uJ/wls+9atrUQi9wZ+wKM26NAk5IiBfd4Lksy8Hi1YUbTp9Gt68lz4qY7Ai/fk6Jw0vnyR+WAw=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:rTYY4fhkDYtz6SJeo0n+sM+0Gvd3/0k1KjtJzKUGLS8","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg5xjAGNcgpxSjh5K/KArdIxgMLULCtBFGf6IsF2nPXyEAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABT